welcome to the Macmillan report I'm  Marilyn Wilkes your host and our guest  is Jessica Weiss assistant professor of  political science and a research fellow  at the Macmillan Center at Yale  University her research interests  include Chinese politics and  international relations nationalism and  social protest before joining the Yale  faculty she founded faces the forum for  American Chinese exchange at Stanford  University she teaches courses on anti  Americanism in world politics Chinese  foreign policy and State Society  relations in post now China today we'll  talk with her about her recent paper  powerful patriots anti-foreign protests  and authoritarian regimes welcome  professor Weiss thank you for having me  so let's talk about the argument you put  forth in your paper  tell us about it well in the paper which  is drawn from an excerpt of my larger  book manuscript I argue that  authoritarian leaders like China's have  strategic incentives to allow  anti-foreign protesters to say popular  demonstrations against foreign  governments and the arguments a little  bit counterintuitive the idea is that  even though protests are risky and have  the potential to turn against the  government and spiral out of control  governments like China's actually find  that these protests are useful in terms  of generating diplomatic bargaining  leverage so that even though protests  are risky it's that risk and the  difficulty of reining them in once they  begin that give leaders sort of domestic  political reasons to claim then on the  international stage that look we're  really here in a corner if we're going  to reach an agreement you the foreign  government need to cut us some slack  okay and what drew you to write this  paper what was your inspiration well in  2005 there were a series of  anti-japanese protests that broke out in  cities across China and it seemed at the  time that Western observers didn't have  let's say a unified view of what was  going on there was a lot of controversy  in particular there are two common views  one suggested that the government had  manufactured these protests either as a  way to rally the population around the  Chinese government to provide citizens  with sort of a safety valve to release  their grievances of whatever sort but  then from a completely opposite view  others suggested that these protests  were actually sort of a spontaneous  eruption of legitimate grievances and  which were quite dangerous to the  government that they actually rather  than being a safety valve could actually  turn against the government if it were  seen as being too weak and defending the  nation's interests so it was clear from  that particular episode which I which I  analyzed in this paper that there was a  gap in our understanding of what these  protests meant and particularly what  they what their implications were for  China's foreign relations okay and let's  talk about your methodology how did you  collect the data for the paper  well mostly I rely upon interviews and a  close reading of Chinese internet  websites I spent over a year in China  living in Beijing doing my field  research and it was clear through these  interviews with nationalist activists  protest organizers participants internet  forum managers Foreign Ministry  officials even a policeman that there  was a pattern that this was not an  isolated incident these 2005  anti-japanese protests and it was clear  that although the Chinese government had  allowed protests nationalist protests in  some cases in 2005 but also following  the u.s. bombing of the Chinese Embassy  in Belgrade in 1999 the Chinese  government had also on a number of  occasions actually restrained students  and others from taking to the streets  such as in 2001 when a US spy plane  collided with a Chinese fighter jet  causing the Chinese plane to ditch into  the sea and the  died you might have expected in fact  some suggested that there were protests  but in fact the Chinese government  prevented protests from occurring and  told the official media to turn down  their rhetoric in covering that incident  similarly on the issue of Taiwan an  issue of great concern to Chinese  nationalists and in the news today  there haven't been to date any protests  in China over the issue of Taiwan and  Taiwan independence so this sort of  created for me a puzzle that I uncovered  as I looked further into the background  of the 2005 protests just two years ago  in the lead up to the Olympics there  were a series of anti French protests so  again there's sort of multi headed  nationalism in China and I think it's is  it a little understood but it's  certainly there's a lot of material out  there and online in Chinese but also  foreign capitals pay great attention so  I looked at a lot of Japanese newspapers  as well  I interviewed Japanese and American  officials throughout the course of my  research I'm curious were you able to  talk to any Chinese officials or was  that not possible just in terms to get  their feedback possibly I did talk to a  number of Chinese officials in different  settings sometimes they would be  visiting United States and I'd have a  chance to talk with them sometimes I've  visited them in this there's a Starbucks  next to the foreign ministry in Beijing  so sometimes I would see them there by  and large they were very open to talking  with me I really in fact you know many  cases they're looking to sort of  showcase China in a good light and part  of that is interacting with the Chinese  I was aware American academics and  others who were interested in the study  of China okay great  why is it important to look at  authoritarian regimes like China an  international conflict well it's funny I  should ask because you know maybe 50  years ago people would talk about  autocracies as having  advantages because they're relatively  opaque it's hard to know what's exactly  going on inside them and by contrast  people with lament the inability of  democracies to conduct foreign policy in  sort of a sort of efficient manner there  is always compromise and negotiations  that would go on before the country  could articulate its foreign policy but  recently scholarship is swung in the  other direction and people have  increasingly touted the advantages of  democracy that that same openness and  giving democracies advantages and all  sorts of things like concluding trade  agreements winning Wars fighting on the  battlefield all sorts of things the  advantages that have been attributed to  democracies and scholarship on the  behavior of autocracy Zin international  relations and international conflict has  in recent years only begun to pick up  and but in this context I the argument  that I put forward essentially levels  the playing field between autocratic  states like China and democratic states  the United States so the argument that I  put forward basically says that if you  could imagine say a democrat an autocrat  sitting at a table negotiating  traditionally you would some might say  that the the Democrat the president of  the Prime Minister could say to the his  autocratic counterpart look Congress has  spoken I've pinned basically I don't  have any flexibility so if we're going  to reach a deal you the autocrat have to  give way and what I'm suggesting is that  the autocrat may not face elections but  nonetheless with mobs and the streets  you can say well you may have Congress  but look at me I've got mobs and we as  we know that the the consequences of  being voted out of office are far less  than perhaps the consequences of the mob  storming the palace gates and  potentially throwing the dictator into  exile or something much worse sure let's  talk about some of the conclusions you  reach in your paper well in the paper  I suggest that the anti-japanese  protests of 2005 we're an effective part  of China's diplomatic strategy to  undermine Japan's bid for  membership on the UN Security Council  and in doing so the Chinese were able to  mount a campaign particularly persuading  those in the region particularly the  United States and nations in Southeast  Asia from tempering their public support  for Japan's bid to be a permanent member  of the Security Council okay and finally  and doing the research for your paper  what was one of the most surprising  things you found well you know people  often assume that because China is an  autocratic state that the people are  more or less fed what the government  wants them to hear there's a sense in  which they aren't particularly  well-informed about foreign affairs and  politics in general and I actually found  that to be absolutely not the case and  in fact Chinese citizens whether it's  you know your students and universities  or you know they're notorious Beijing  taxi cab drivers they know a lot in fact  they I would wager that they know more  about international relations than sort  of their American equivalents and so  even though there's may perhaps less  space for political discourse  nevertheless the amount of information  in their knowledge of world affairs is  really quite striking interesting thank  you very much for being here with us  today and sharing some of your research  you're welcome for more information  about professor Weiss and her work  please visit our website at Yale edu  backslash Macmillan report be sure to  join us again for another episode of the  Macmillan report made possible through  funding from the Whitney and Betty  Macmillan Centre for International and  area studies at Yale  you
 

